A||||4||RFMike||How do you know the right tire size
Z||000000||RFMike||08-18-99||07:02 PM||||My 86 1/2 GT has 205-15's all the way around. I read the inside door panel, and it calls for 205-15's front, 215-15's back.  I know that some were made each way.  I had my cop friend radar me, and the speedo is more accurate than the usual margin of error.  The odometer is also accurate.  My mileage is better than most figures given in other posts, and it accelerates hard.  Is there any other way to check?  I know if I go to a bigger diameter, actual mileage increases, but how much will I lose off the line?  This is my daily driver through rush hour, but I take it on road trips, too.
Z||000001||batboy||08-18-99||09:27 PM||||Going up or down one or sometimes even two tire size usually isn't too noticeable to performance, gas mileage, and maybe only a MPH or two difference on the speedo.
Z||000002||Speadraser||08-19-99||04:05 PM||||Okay I'm gonna give you two formulas.  One will give you a calculated tire width, height(off the rim), and total diameter.  The second will give you  RPM's for a given final drive ratio and MPH.  Here goes.<p>205/50R15<p>-205 is the width in milimeters<p>-you didn't mention the aspect ratio I'll assume it's 50<p>-R means it's a radial tire<p>-15 of course means the rim diameter<p>205 / 25.4 = Width in inches(8.07)<p>take that dividend and multiply it by the aspect ratio in percent(4.03)<p>multiply the product by 2 equaling the number of sidewalls(8.07)<p>then add the diameter of the rim.(23.07)<br>the answer is in inches.<p><p>now for the second formula.<p>336 x a x b<br>-----------  =  R<br>      d<p>-336 is a constant<p>-a is the MPH you wish to input<p>-b is the final drive ratio<p>-d is the tire diameter<p>-and R is the RPM<p>I'm not sure whether you have a 5 speed or an automatic and I believe there were two final drives for the auto. So I'll pick the one in the middle(3.18)<p>we'll start with 60 MPH times 336 times you final drive(64108.8)<p>divide the product by the tire diameter in inches<p>this equals your predicted and calculated RPM(2778.8) this doesn't take into account slippage from your torque converter, but that wouldn't apply to us Fiero owners!<br>Hope this helps.  And thats all for our algebra lesson for today...<br>Speady
Z||000003||theogre||08-19-99||07:42 PM||||one of these days I have to colect all the tire stuff..... it's spread all over the place.<p>BTW. Another point that confuses people. The width of the tire printed on it is Not it's tread width. It's the width thru the widest point in the sidewalls when mounted on a specific test rim. <p>Tires of the same size can have a range of tread width on them. Always check out the actual tread width before you get them. ((Tread width is not printer on the tire at all)) You don't want to spend allot of money only to get a tire .25 inch wider in the tread than the 195's. (There's like an inch range from widest to narrowest on the 195's I've looked at.)
Z||000004||RFMike||08-19-99||08:04 PM||||Speedraser,<br>I have the four speed, and I think the final drive ratio is 3.65:1.  The tires I have are 205/60R15.  If I go through your calcs, I will get a calculated speed reading for a given tire size.  This doesn't tell me whether the speedo is right or wrong, or the tires are too large or the right size.  The tires could be the right size, and the speedo could be wrong, or the speedo wrong and the tires right.<br>I guess I will have to decide if I want to give up an unknown amount of performance in exchange for an unknown savings in fuel economy.  Ga-rate.  <br>I'm also unsure what it will look like with the larger tires in the rear.  Anybody out there have the 205/60r15 front, 215/60r15 back factory setup?<br>Ogre, or anyone else, Dunlop has a very good site for tire information.  Tempted to copy some of it here, but I won't.  Not everybody is as interested in minutiae as me.
